; Tutorial_6_Convert_Again.inix -- a spoken walk through BatCon, written for buildTutorials (the format is
; described at the top of Tutorial_0_Overview.inix in the Homer kit's Templates).

[about]
Title=6 - Convert Again
Intro=Running a batch a second time: what is kept, and how to replace it safely.
Setup=BatCon is open, and a batch has been converted once.
Homework=Convert the same batch twice, once with Force replacements.

[step]
Say=Sooner or later you convert the same files again: the sources changed, or you want the newest conversion. BatCon is careful with what is already there.

[step]
Say=Run the same batch a second time, as it was. Enter starts it.
Key=Enter
Hear=BatCon results

[step]
Say=Every result already there is kept as it was, and the summary says so.
Hear=0 files converted, 3 kept as they were, 0 skipped for want of a conversion, 0 failed.

[step]
Say=Down Arrow reads why each was kept.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=Skipped Budget dot m d: it is already there.

[step]
Say=That is the safe choice: nothing you made earlier, or edited since, is replaced unless you ask. Escape closes the Results box.
Key=Escape
Note=The reader says nothing as the window closes.

[step]
Say=To replace the results, open BatCon again, and check Force replacements: Alt plus F, Foxtrot.
Key=Alt+F
Hear=Force replacements, check box, checked

[step]
Say=Press Enter. This time every file is converted again.
Key=Enter
Hear=Converting 3 files to md
Hear=3 files converted, 0 kept as they were, 0 skipped for want of a conversion, 0 failed.

[step]
Say=Even with Force replacements, an earlier result is replaced only once its new conversion has worked. Each conversion is written to a temporary file first, and put in place only when it succeeds, so a failure never loses what you had.

[step]
Say=A source is never overwritten either. If a conversion's name would match a source's, BatCon gives the result a new name instead.

[step]
Say=Force replacements stays checked only for this run, unless Use configuration is checked too, so you cannot replace results by surprise next time.
Key=Alt+F
Hear=Force replacements, check box, not checked

[step]
Say=A good habit when the sources change: convert into a fresh output folder rather than replacing. The old results stay as they were, and you can compare the two.
Key=Alt+O
Hear=Output directory: edit, C colon backslash Reports

[step]
Say=Force replacements is also a switch on the command line, slash force, for a script that rebuilds every result each night. Walk 7 shows the command line.

[step]
Say=Whatever you choose, the Results box says what happened to each file, so a run that kept everything never looks like one that did nothing.
Hear=BatCon results

[step]
Say=Kept results are not a failure, and the exit number for a script stays zero when everything was either converted or kept, since nothing went wrong.
Hear=0 files converted, 3 kept as they were, 0 skipped for want of a conversion, 0 failed.

[step]
Say=If you want only the files that changed converted, put the changed ones alone in Source files, or convert into a new output folder and compare; BatCon does not judge which source is newer than its result.

[step]
Say=Default settings clears Force replacements along with everything else, so it is always safe to start over from there.
Key=Alt+F
Hear=Force replacements, check box, not checked

[step]
Say=What this walk taught: results already there are kept by default, Alt plus F replaces them, and a replacement happens only once its new conversion has worked.
